<p class="lt-chem-154798">For an analyte that is not easy to oxidize or reduce, we can complete a coulometric titration by coupling a mediator&rsquo;s oxidation or reduction to an acid&ndash;base, precipitation, or complexation reaction that involves the analyte. For example, if we use H<sub>2</sub>O as a mediator, we can generate H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>at the anode</p>

<p class="lt-chem-154798">\[6 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}(l) \rightleftharpoons 4 \mathrm{H}_{3} \text{O}^{+}(a q)+\text{ O}_{2}(g)+4 e^{-} \nonumber\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-154798">and generate OH<sup>&ndash;</sup> at the cathode.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-154798">\[2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}(l)+2 e^{-} \rightleftharpoons 2 \mathrm{OH}^{-}(a q)+\text{ H}_{2}(g) \nonumber\]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-154798">If we carry out the oxidation or reduction of H<sub>2</sub>O using the generator cell in <a title="11.3: Coulometric Methods" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/11%3A_Electrochemical_Methods/11.03%3A_Coulometric_Methods#figure11.3.7" rel="internal">Figure 11.3.7
</a>, then we can selectively dispense H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> or OH<sup>&ndash;</sup> into a solution that contains the analyte. The resulting reaction is identical to that in an acid&ndash;base titration. Coulometric acid&ndash;base titrations have been used for the analysis of strong and weak acids and bases, in both aqueous and non-aqueous matrices. Table 11.3.3
 summarizes several examples of coulometric titrations that involve acid&ndash;base, complexation, and precipitation reactions.</p>

